      "type": "text",
      "text": "Ok so what is ncurses right that should be the first topic on our agenda. Ncurses really simply is a clibrary for creating text-based user interfaces in the terminal.
      ok to start, ncurses has a concept of these things called windows and these other things called screens. TO simplify things quite a bit a window is a data struccture that contains what you see on the screen and the screen is what you actually write to

      you can get really complicated iwth a bucn of windows and screens or whatever but really most situations you just have a buffer window that wrights to a terminal screen that is what is currently being written
      to the screen.

      Ncuerses gives us 2 tools to start to make these screen routines easy

      curscreen and stdscr curscreen gets the terminal screen and stdscr is a pprovided as a blank canvas for us to make edits to. A window is like a scratch pad or a write buffer. To amake the section (or usually the whole) of the screen correspond to a window
      use the routine refresh or wrefresh. If we are not suing the srdscreen use w in front of the function btw, this is a standard convention for when we are applying a operation to a specific window.

      so curscr: what terminlal looks like
      stdscr: what the terminal screen should look like next

      you should (basically) never actually access curscr directly changes should be made through the API(the set of functions provided by the ncurses library)

      ok so after you change stdscr you do refresh or wrefresh and that is how you get the output that you actually want. Many function are just defined to use the stdscr as a default

      for example, this is addch which adds a character to the stdscr. If we wanted to use a different sceen we would use waddch

      I said earlier but unless a call is one wherea window must be specified then if you want to specift a window you gotta add a w in front of the function call.


      In order for the current (y, x) (ncurses uses (y, x) not (x, y)) to move from one point to aother move and wmove are provided. it is often desirbnale to move then perform some IUO function. to avoid clumsiness most IO functions can be prefixed with mv and the desired (y,x) as a paramater to specify where to move to.

      example:
      move(y, x);
      addch(ch);

      is the same as

      mvaddch(y, x, ch);

      you can also use the w prefix to use a window instead of the stdscr. for example, wmvaddch().

      btw the w always comes before the mv.

      ok jsut some other things to note about ncurses. it automically defines booleans so thats cool.

      ALSO ERR and OK let you know if a function call was successful or not. ERR is -1 and OK is 0.

